# Allowlist would be wrong here and a denylist is right, which is the
# opposite of the repository root's .dockerignore -- worth saying why.
#
# The backend image needs two paths out of a tree that also holds a .env, so
# there the safe default is "nothing, then add back". This build needs almost
# the whole package: every source file, both tsconfigs, the eslint and vite
# configs, the SDL and the generated types. Listing those by hand would be a
# second inventory to keep in step with the build, and forgetting an entry
# fails as a TypeScript error three minutes into a build rather than as an
# obviously missing file.
#
# What is excluded is what the build produces or reinstalls for itself.

# Installed by `npm ci` inside the builder. Copying a host node_modules would
# ship Windows-resolved optional dependencies into a Linux image -- esbuild
# and rollup pick platform-specific binaries -- and would make the build
# depend on whatever is on the developer's disk rather than on the lockfile.
node_modules

# The previous build's output. `vite build` writes it; copying a stale one in
# would let a successful-looking image serve the bundle from last week.
dist

# Never any use to a build, and .env is the one file in this repository that
# holds a real credential.
.env
.env.*
.git

# The Playwright suite, which drives the *built* image from outside it and is
# in no tsconfig, so `tsc -b && vite build` never reads a byte of it. Excluded
# for the layer cache rather than for size: without this, editing a spec
# invalidates `COPY . .` and forces a full rebuild of a bundle that could not
# have changed.
e2e
